(23, 'The target is `flip_case`. I’m choosing the direct Python operation `string.swapcase()`; it is valid for the full Python string contract (including non-ASCII case mappings) and keeps the handwritten semantics focused: function definition/call, name lookup, attribute dispatch, and return. The proof can then remain symbolic over every K `String`, instead of merely enumerating examples.')
(61, 'The first semantics draft is now in place. It parses the emitted constructors rather than bypassing `solution.mpy`, installs the function, binds its argument, evaluates name/attribute/call/return nodes, and exposes a symbolic postcondition. I’m compiling it now; K’s grammar and cell-pattern checks are usually where small definition issues surface.')
(147, 'The two claims now close with `#Top`, including the unconstrained symbolic-string theorem and the prompt example. I caught and corrected a K claim-shaping issue: reachability rewrites belong inside the cells, which avoids a frontend crash on whole-configuration rewrites. Before finalizing, I’m tightening the `swapcase` primitive so its executable behavior matches Python’s Unicode behavior, not only the ASCII test corpus.')
(197, 'The Unicode execution path now matches CPython, including multi-character expansions such as `ß → SS`. Because this K release’s string hook exposes non-ASCII text as UTF-8 bytes, the semantics explicitly decodes one UTF-8 scalar at a time and uses generated equations for all 2,816 case-mapped Unicode code points. The strengthened Unicode end-to-end claim also closes with `#Top`.')
